          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Political Challenges and Emigration</strong><br>\nBorn faced significant challenges during the rise of the Nazi regime in Germany because of his Jewish heritage. In 1933, he was forced to leave Germany, emigrating first to Britain. This period was difficult for Born as he had to rebuild his life and career in a new country amidst growing political tensions in Europe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Academic Life in Britain</strong><br>\nIn Britain, Max Born continued to work on theoretical physics at the University of Cambridge and later at the University of Edinburgh, where he remained until his retirement in 1953. During his time in Britain, he maintained his prolific output of scientific papers and books, including his influential work \"Natural Philosophy of Cause and Chance.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration and Mentoring</strong><br>\nThroughout his career, Born collaborated with several other prominent scientists, such as Werner Heisenberg and Niels Bohr. He was also known for his dedication to teaching and mentoring, influencing a generation of physicists. His students included future Nobel laureates, reflecting the impact of his guidance on the field of physics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
