          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Philosophical Views on Science</strong><br>\nBeyond his mathematical and physical theories, Born was deeply interested in the philosophical aspects of science. He believed that physics should not only advance in terms of technical developments but also reflect on the implications of scientific discoveries on human thought and society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nMax Born was married to Hedwig Ehrenberg, with whom he had three children. His family life was marked by the turmoil of the times, but he also enjoyed a close relationship with his grandchildren. Born's legacy includes not only his scientific contributions but also his advocacy for responsible use of scientific knowledge, especially concerning nuclear weapons after World War II.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nMax Born passed away on January 5, 1970, in Göttingen, Germany. His death marked the loss of one of the pioneers of quantum mechanics. However, his work continues to influence contemporary physics and mathematics. Posthumously, his research and publications remain a subject of study and respect, showcasing the lasting importance of his contributions to science.</p>\n"
              },
